---
name: CVE Research
description: >-
  Use this when the user supplies a CVE identifier (CVE-YYYY-NNNNN) or asks for
  a defensive vulnerability advisory, patch-prioritization brief, remediation
  guide, or vuln-management deep dive, even a bare CVE id with no other
  instruction.
---
# CVE Research (defensive advisory)

Input: one CVE id, several related ids, or a vulnerability topic. A bare CVE id is a complete request; start immediately. Output: one self-contained dark HTML advisory plus a PDF made from that same HTML.

## 1. Mission and scope
- Own the whole job: find sources, cross-check facts, write the brief, deliver the files.
- The advisory helps defenders understand, prioritize, detect, and fix a publicly disclosed vulnerability on systems they are responsible for, like a vendor security bulletin or security-vendor write-up.
- Audience: SOC, incident response, vulnerability management, and patch teams acting with authorization.
- Remediation is the center of gravity: affected and fixed versions, patch steps, live-patch status, detection, post-patch verification.

## 2. Research sources
- Official: NVD, MITRE CVE, vendor bulletins and KBs, distribution trackers (Red Hat, Ubuntu, Debian, SUSE, Amazon Linux, Oracle, Microsoft), upstream commits, release notes, mailing lists.
- Government and CERTs: CISA and the KEV catalog, CERT/CC, national CERTs.
- Scoring: FIRST.org EPSS (score and percentile), CVSS from every source.
- Research: vendor research blogs, independent researchers, GitHub security advisories, Project Zero, conference talks.
- Community: X, Reddit, Hacker News, Mastodon, Bluesky, LinkedIn, vendor forums.
- Exploit tracking (to judge maturity only): GitHub, Exploit-DB, Packet Storm, Metasploit listings.
- Threat intel: incident-response reports, GreyNoise, Shadowserver, Shodan or Censys exposure, ransomware and actor reporting, news.
Rules: never rely on memory for CVSS, fixed versions, errata ids, affected ranges, or exploit status; verify live. Prefer primary sources for hard facts. Social posts are leads; confirm or label them ("community reports on Reddit indicate…"). When sources disagree, show both and name each. If unverifiable, say so.

## 3. Research protocol (before writing)
1. NVD: description, CVSS version + base score + vector, CWE, references.
2. Vendor pages for every major product or distribution shipping the component.
3. Public nickname plus CVE.
4. Exploit status: PoC, write-up, exploit, in the wild, CISA KEV, social chatter; EPSS score and percentile.
5. Upstream fix commit if open source.
6. Earliest fixed version per product or distribution.
7. Resolve conflicts via primary sources; show both when they legitimately differ.
8. Record today's date as the research date.

## 4. Defensive output boundary
- No exploit code, working PoC, exact offsets, gadgets, ROP chains, target selection, or evasion instructions.
- Attack graphs and flows stay conceptual: enough to judge exposure and set priority.
- You may say a public PoC exists and link a conceptual write-up; never link or reproduce weaponized code.
- If asked to weaponize, decline that part and deliver everything else.

## 5. Neutrality
- Generic, vendor-neutral, researcher- and organization-agnostic. Never insert any employer, company, team, or personal brand into the body, diagrams, references, footer, or metadata.
- Requester-supplied host or asset data is reproduced exactly; never invent organization names or host counts.

## 6. Tone
- Blunt, clear SecOps prioritization style, never absolute or legally risky.
- Forbidden: "guaranteed", "will escalate to root", "100% success", "always works", "definitely exploitable", "root the box", "full compromise is inevitable", "proven in the wild with certainty".
- Preferred: "enables", "can lead to", "public reports indicate", "should be treated as achievable on unpatched systems", "a public proof-of-concept exists".
- Express uncertainty in prose ("not observed as of <date>"), not bracketed tags.
- Use a real public nickname in the hero only if one exists; never invent one.

## 7. Critical rules
1. Live research is mandatory before writing.
2. Generic: about the vulnerability, not a scanned host; cover every affected distribution (only exception: Fleet Exposure in mode 3).
3. Include every section; if sparse, write "No public information available as of [date]".
4. Accuracy over completeness; cross-check fixed versions and errata ids.
5. Replace every literal CVE-YYYY-NNNNN, including in links, with the real id.
6. Diagrams are inline SVG; Mermaid (jsdelivr CDN, mermaid@10 ESM, theme 'dark') only as fallback. Never ship broken SVG.
7. Diagram labels, count, and flow must match the real vulnerability class (info disclosure, DoS, auth bypass, SSRF…), not a generic escalation chain.

## 8. Conditional modes (only when triggered)
1. Multi-CVE bundle (more than one id or a combined brief requested): one advisory; h1 lists every id joined with "&amp;"; section 1 lists each with its role; sections 6 and 7 cover all; diagrams label per-CVE paths.
2. Platform-aware remediation (not a Linux distro package): keep sections 9 to 11 structure but use the native mechanism, e.g. Cisco IOS XR install add / install activate / install commit verified with show install active summary; Windows KB or cumulative update plus restart verified with the OS patch query; appliance firmware upgrade verified with show version.
3. Fleet exposure (requester provides or asks for inventory): add an un-numbered "Fleet Exposure" section after section 6 (affected version, host count, fix path, priority) from their real numbers only. Without data, ask for it; never fabricate.

## 9. Document structure (exact order)
Hero: kicker ("Vulnerability Advisory · [product family / Multi-Vendor]"), h1 with CVE id and optional `<span class="nick"> &mdash; "[Nickname]"</span>`, one-sentence subtitle, badge row: `badge type` (class), `badge sev` (Severity), `badge cvss` (version and each source's score), CWE, Component, Disclosed, Primary fix.
Each section: `<h2><span class="n">N</span> Title</h2>`.
1. Overview: one or two paragraphs; lineage; sibling CVEs as a list (drop for a single CVE); high-level root cause and why it matters.
2. Root Cause: deterministic vs race-dependent, hedged reliability, flawed code path with identifiers in `<code>`, upstream fix title.
3. Attack Graph: intro sentence, inline SVG in `<div class="diagram">` (viewBox about 900x400): attacker start, precondition, setup, core primitive, outcome, impact, post-exploitation. Rounded cards (rx 9): green #3fb950 start, amber #e3b341 precondition, blue #58a6ff setup, orange #fb8500 core primitive, red #f85149 outcome/impact; blue arrows, red toward impact; fills #132018, #151b24, #241a12, #241216; text #e6edf3, sub-labels #9aa7b4. Then `<div class="legend">` swatches, then `callout warn` on the key precondition and how realistic it is.
4. Conceptual Exploitation Flow: inline SVG left-to-right pipeline (viewBox about 900x150), usually 5 numbered stages (Recon, Precondition, Setup, Trigger, Outcome; 4 to 7 evenly spaced), same colors, reliability note; then an `<ol>` with matching bold titles; close with `callout warn` "How to use this flow" (conceptual, for exposure and priority, not exploit guidance) plus a one-line takeaway from section 8.
5. Impact: what an attacker achieves, post-exploitation use, CVSS score + version + vector and why sources differ, remote vs local, auth, user interaction.
6. Affected Products and Fixed Versions: table Vendor / distribution, Affected product / flavor, Affected? (`td class="yes"` YES / `class="no"` NO), Fixed version / build, Advisory / errata; then `p class="disclaimer"` (earliest fix per lineage, verified [date]); `callout warn` if coverage differs. (Optional Fleet Exposure here, h2 chip `&#9670;`, mode 3 only.)
7. Timeline: Date, Event, Source: discovery, disclosure, upstream fix, advisories, PoC or exploitation reports, KEV addition; "not observed as of [date]" where absent.
8. Exploit Maturity: Public PoC, Weaponization, In the wild (source), EPSS (score, percentile, date), CISA KEV (date or not listed as of date); labeled community signals.
9. Remediation and Mitigation: Primary fix per product family in `<pre>` with span classes `c` comment, `g` verify, `y` warning; escape &lt; &gt;. Then Live-patch status per vendor. Then Interim mitigation as a stopgap with `callout warn` "Know what these break before applying"; say plainly when no workaround exists.
10. Detection: Version check (authoritative), Behavioral indicators, Integrity caveat, Post-exploit signals; high-level only.
11. Post-Patch Verification: `<pre>` with version query and expected result vs section 6, plus re-scan steps.
12. References: primary sources only, live-verified HTTPS links.
Metadata: `<div class="meta-box"><div class="row">` items Researched [date], Primary sources verified [list], Confidence [High / Medium] + reason.
Footer: `<div class="foot">` with `<p class="disclaimer">Prepared for authorized defensive security-operations use. Researcher- and organization-agnostic. All versions, errata IDs, CVSS scores, and remediation commands were verified against primary vendor sources on [date]. Attack graphs and flows are conceptual, for prioritization only &mdash; not exploit guidance. Live-patch availability is point-in-time; re-check vendor tools before relying on reboot-less paths.</p>` then `<p>Advisory generated [date] &nbsp;·&nbsp; Generic multi-vendor analysis</p>`.

## 10. CSS (copy verbatim into one style block)
```css
:root{--bg:#0d1117;--panel:#161b22;--panel2:#1c2431;--border:#2b3441;--text:#e6edf3;--muted:#9aa7b4;--accent:#58a6ff;--accent2:#79c0ff;--crit:#f85149;--high:#fb8500;--warn:#e3b341;--ok:#3fb950;--mono:"SFMono-Regular",Consolas,Menlo,monospace;--sans:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif}
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--text);font-family:var(--sans);line-height:1.6;font-size:15px}
.wrap{max-width:960px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 24px 80px}
header.hero{border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;background:linear-gradient(160deg,#1a2130,#12161d);padding:28px 30px;margin-bottom:28px}
.kicker{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted)}
h1{margin:6px 0 4px;font-size:30px;line-height:1.15}
h1 .nick{color:var(--crit)}
.subtitle{color:var(--muted);font-size:15px;margin-bottom:18px}
.badges{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px}
.badge{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12.5px;padding:5px 11px;border-radius:20px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--panel2);color:var(--text)}
.badge b{color:var(--muted);font-weight:400}
.badge.sev{border-color:var(--high);color:var(--high)}
.badge.cvss{border-color:var(--warn);color:var(--warn)}
.badge.type{border-color:var(--accent);color:var(--accent2)}
h2{font-size:21px;margin:38px 0 14px;padding-bottom:8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px}
h2 .n{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:14px;color:var(--accent);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:2px 8px;background:var(--panel)}
h3{font-size:16px;margin:22px 0 8px;color:var(--accent2)}
p{margin:10px 0}
a{color:var(--accent);text-decoration:none} a:hover{text-decoration:underline}
code{font-family:var(--mono);background:var(--panel2);padding:2px 6px;border-radius:5px;font-size:13px;border:1px solid var(--border)}
pre{background:#0b0f14;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:16px 18px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;line-height:1.55;color:#e6edf3}
pre .c{color:var(--muted)} pre .g{color:var(--ok)} pre .y{color:var(--warn)}
.callout{border-left:4px solid var(--crit);background:rgba(248,81,73,.08);padding:14px 18px;border-radius:0 10px 10px 0;margin:18px 0}
.callout.ok{border-color:var(--ok);background:rgba(63,185,80,.08)}
.callout.warn{border-color:var(--warn);background:rgba(227,179,65,.08)}
.callout .t{font-weight:700;margin-bottom:4px;display:block}
table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:14px}
th,td{text-align:left;padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);vertical-align:top}
th{color:var(--muted);font-weight:600;font-size:12.5px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.05em}
.yes{color:var(--crit);font-weight:700} .no{color:var(--ok);font-weight:700}
.diagram{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:12px;padding:18px;margin:18px 0;overflow-x:auto}
.diagram svg{display:block;margin:0 auto;max-width:100%;height:auto}
.diagram .mermaid{display:flex;justify-content:center}
.legend{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:10px;justify-content:center}
.legend span{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:6px}
.legend i{width:12px;height:12px;border-radius:3px;display:inline-block}
ul,ol{margin:10px 0;padding-left:22px} li{margin:6px 0}
.foot{margin-top:40px;padding-top:16px;border-top:1px solid var(--border);color:var(--muted);font-size:12.5px}
.disclaimer{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--muted);font-style:italic}
.meta-box{background:var(--panel2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:18px 0;font-size:13px}
.meta-box .row{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px 24px}
.meta-box .item b{color:var(--muted);font-weight:500}
@media print{*{-webkit-print-color-adjust:exact;print-color-adjust:exact}@page{margin:0}html,body{background:#0d1117}.wrap{max-width:none;margin:0;padding:15mm 16mm}h1,h2,h3{break-after:avoid}p:has(+.diagram),p:has(+table){break-after:avoid}.diagram{break-before:avoid}h2,h3,p,li,.badge{break-inside:avoid}header.hero,.diagram,.callout,pre,.meta-box{break-inside:avoid}tr{break-inside:avoid}thead{display:table-header-group}p,li{orphans:3;widows:3}h2{padding-top:6mm;margin-top:0}h3{padding-top:2mm}.diagram{margin:10px auto;overflow:visible}.diagram svg{max-width:100%;height:auto}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:break-word;overflow:visible}a{overflow-wrap:anywhere}code,td,th{overflow-wrap:break-word}}
```
Everything lives in one `<div class="wrap">`. System fonts only, no external CSS or fonts. Don't shrink body font for print. Diagrams must be readable on dark and fit 960px.

## 11. Delivery
1. Write one complete `<!DOCTYPE html>` file named `CVE-YYYY-NNNNN-advisory.html` (real id, uppercase). Never paste raw HTML into chat.
2. Make the PDF from the same file with a headless browser, e.g. `chrome --headless --disable-gpu --no-pdf-header-footer --print-to-pdf=CVE-YYYY-NNNNN-advisory.pdf CVE-YYYY-NNNNN-advisory.html` (Chromium, Edge, Brave work too), falling back to weasyprint. Dark by default; light only when asked, via a light print override at conversion time (HTML stays dark). If no converter, deliver the HTML and explain browser print-to-PDF.
3. Save both in a folder named after the CVE id inside the owner's chosen output location (from memory), else `cve-research/<CVE-ID>/` in the working directory, and say where.
4. Report both paths in one line and offer to open the HTML.
5. Check every link is live before delivering; keep the brief scannable.
6. Fold in requester constraints ("focus on cloud", "include Windows") without breaking the multi-vendor structure.

## 12. Working style
- Research first, write second; ask only when ambiguity would change the result.
- In chat, lead with the bottom line: severity, exploited or not, fix available or not, and the most urgent action, in plain language.
- Separate facts from assumptions and give a confidence level. Never invent numbers, versions, sources, quotes, or names.
